From d76b8de5709ff948ea025985312182963fea34d8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 00:49:23 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] CDVDDemuxFFmpeg: a VC-1 stream of a file of MPEG-TS opens at
 the rate its header declares, not twice it

FFmpeg gives a VC-1 stream twice the rate its sequence header declares
until its analysis has read the third picture and its own codec context
has read the header (BlurayVc1Rate.h). A Blu-ray takes the declared rate
where the disc's playlist declares it too. A file of MPEG-TS played on its
own (a .m2ts copied from a disc, a .ts) has no playlist, and one without
HD audio is analysed for half a second and opened again
(CDVDDemuxFFmpeg::Open()): the streams of the second opening are built
before FFmpeg has read anything of them, with the frame rates the first
opening found but without the rate the header declares. The player
chooses the display mode as it opens the first video stream, from those
streams. Measured with Hades' FFmpeg 9.0.2 on the 13 synthetic VC-1
clips, each opened as CDVDDemuxFFmpeg::Open() opens a file: the streams
of the second opening were at twice the rate for 12 of them (47.952 for
23.976, 50 for 25, 59.94 for 29.97, 48 for 24, 100 for 50), and 8 stayed
so when the first video packet built them again.

Now the second opening puts back the rate the header of a VC-1 stream
declares, and for a VC-1 stream of a file that FFmpeg reads with its
MPEG-TS demuxer, whose rate from FFmpeg is exactly twice the declared one
and whose average rate is either not known or the declared one, the
declared rate is taken: the one FFmpeg gives once it has read three
pictures and the header. A stream whose average is another rate, a VC-1
stream in any other container (Matroska gives the duration of each
picture) and any other codec are as before; so is a Blu-ray, where the
average that is the declared rate never comes with twice it. Said in the
log when it is taken.
